🏗️ First, the big picture

OSI Model = 7 layers (the "theoretical textbook" version — very detailed, made for understanding concepts)
TCP/IP Model = 4 layers (the "practical real-world" version — what the internet ACTUALLY uses)

Think of OSI as the detailed recipe with every tiny step, and TCP/IP as the simplified version your mom actually cooks with 😂

📊 Side-by-side Mapping
OSI (7 layers)	What happens here	TCP/IP (4 layers)
7️⃣ Application	Apps talk to network (HTTP, FTP, DNS, Email)	🟦 Application
6️⃣ Presentation	Data formatting, encryption, compression (SSL/TLS)	🟦 Application
5️⃣ Session	Opens/manages/closes a "conversation" between devices	🟦 Application
4️⃣ Transport	Reliable delivery — TCP (safe) or UDP (fast)	🟩 Transport
3️⃣ Network	Routing — deciding the BEST path (IP addresses live here)	🟨 Internet
2️⃣ Data Link	Device-to-device delivery on same network (MAC address, switches)	🟥 Network Access
1️⃣ Physical	Actual cables, wifi signals, electricity, light pulses	🟥 Network Access
🎬 Real-life analogy: Sending a Love Letter 💌 to your gf
Layer	What it's like
Application	You write the actual message: "I miss you" 💕
Presentation	You translate it to her language / seal it in a fancy envelope (encryption)
Session	You start a "call" — establish that a conversation is happening
Transport	You choose: Registered Post (TCP - guaranteed, tracked) or just drop it in a random mailbox (UDP - fast, no guarantee)
Network	Post office decides the BEST route/postman path to her city (IP addressing/routing)
Data Link	Local postman delivers letter to the correct house on your street (MAC address)
Physical	The actual bike/vehicle/road the postman travels on 🚲
🧠 Why does TCP/IP merge them?

Because in real life, nobody actually treats Application, Presentation, and Session as 3 separate "jobs" — that's overkill. TCP/IP just says "eh, that's all basically the same layer — app stuff" and merges it. Similarly Data Link + Physical = "getting data physically from point A to B" so they merge into Network Access.

🥡 One-liner to remember FOREVER:

"OSI is the professor's whiteboard version, TCP/IP is what the internet actually runs on."

And for the layer order (bottom to top), remember this cheesy phrase:

"Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away" 🍕
Physical → Data Link → Network → Transport → Session → Presentation → Application